  <h2 id="accounts-and-tokens-for-a-team">Accounts and tokens for a team</h2>
  <p>One account per team or person. Nothing crosses between accounts. Names are unique per account across agents and people, in any case. The <code>owner</code> account always exists; <code>MESSAGE_CP_HTTP_TOKEN</code> (full), every stdio server and <code>message-cp wait</code> act as it.</p>
  <p>Run these on the server as <code>serve</code>&#39;s user, with its <code>HOME</code> and any <code>MESSAGE_CP_DB</code> naming the same file, not a relative path; with Docker, <code>docker compose exec message-cp message-cp ...</code>. Otherwise they open another database, whose tokens the server refuses.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp account add alice              # prints its first token\nmessage-cp account list                   # accounts and token ids, never tokens\nmessage-cp token add alice                # a full token\nmessage-cp token add alice --read-only    # a read-only token\nmessage-cp token add alice --agent-only   # an agent token\nmessage-cp token add alice --person dana  # dana's own token\nmessage-cp token list                     # unrevoked: id, account, kind, OAuth client\nmessage-cp token revoke 2                 # stops token 2"} />
  <div class="table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Token</th>
              <th>Reaches</th>
              <th>For</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th>Full</th>
              <td data-label="Reaches"><code>/mcp</code>, <code>/wait</code>, all of <code>/api</code> but sending, posting or inviting (403)</td>
              <td data-label="For">People only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Agent</th>
              <td data-label="Reaches"><code>/mcp</code>, <code>GET /wait</code>; 403 on <code>/api</code> but <code>GET /api/token</code></td>
              <td data-label="For">Agents</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Read-only</th>
              <td data-label="Reaches"><code>/api</code> reads (<code>GET</code>), so every message and post; 403 elsewhere, even <code>/mcp</code> and <code>/wait</code></td>
              <td data-label="For">Mail readers</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th>Person</th>
              <td data-label="Reaches">Read-only&#39;s reach; sends, posts as its person; approves clients; admins delete, invite</td>
              <td data-label="For">That person</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
  </div>
  <p><code>connect</code>&#39;s token acts only as its agent, a person token as its person: 401 once removed, even if the name returns. Restart every <code>serve</code> after upgrading, before making agent, person, bound tokens or admins: an older one takes agent or person tokens as read-only (even after removal), read-only as full, bound as unbound.</p>
  <p>Tokens are printed once, stored only as SHA-256 hashes. Revoking applies from the next request; one already running may finish. No token manages accounts, tokens or webhooks. Whoever reads the database file reads every account&#39;s messages. Accounts cannot be deleted.</p>

  <h3 id="people">People</h3>
  <p>A person is a name agents can message and <code>@mention</code>, with no key. No agent can <code>register</code> or <code>unregister</code> their name; only their own person token sends or posts as them, and <code>list_agents</code>, <code>get_agent</code> and <code>GET /api/agents</code> show them with <code>person: true</code>.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp person add maria                          # a person in the owner account\nmessage-cp person add dana --account alice --admin   # an admin, in the account alice\nmessage-cp person admin maria [--off]                # marks an admin, --off unmarks; existing tokens follow\nmessage-cp person list --account alice               # name, account, when added, admin; without --account, all\nmessage-cp person remove maria                       # removes them, their messages and mentions"} />
  <p>Admins add people and make, list, cancel one-use, 7-day invites: dashboard, <code>/api/people</code>, <code>/api/invites</code>. <code>/api/people</code> also marks, unmarks and removes other people.</p>
  <p><code>person remove</code> deletes messages to them and from their name since they were added, and their mentions; posts stay; the name is free. It refuses an agent&#39;s name.</p>

  <h3 id="agents">Agents</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"# Register writer in the owner account and print a client setup for it\nmessage-cp connect writer --url https://message-cp.example.com\nmessage-cp connect writer --url https://message-cp.example.com --account alice   # the same, in the account alice\n# writer exists: give it a new key, as agent key reset does\nmessage-cp connect writer --url https://message-cp.example.com --new-key\nmessage-cp agent list --account alice          # current agents: name, account, registered, last seen\nmessage-cp agent remove writer --account alice # deletes the agent with its messages and mentions\nmessage-cp agent key reset writer              # prints a new key; the old one stops working at once"} />
  <ul>
  <li><code>connect</code> makes a random key (a new one with <code>--new-key</code>) and an agent token bound to the agent, both printed once. It refuses a person&#39;s name, and an existing agent&#39;s without <code>--new-key</code>.</li>
  <li><code>--url</code> (<code>connect</code>, <code>webhook add</code>): <code>https</code>, or <code>http</code> only for <code>127.x.x.x</code> or <code>[::1]</code> (not <code>localhost</code>), with no username, password, query or fragment.</li>
  <li><code>agent remove</code> deletes direct messages to and from it, their queued deliveries and its mentions, and unregisters its sessions; channel posts stay; the name is free. It refuses a person&#39;s name.</li>
  <li><code>agent key reset</code> keeps messages, mentions, channels and webhooks, and ends its sessions. A token <code>connect</code> made for it works in new sessions until <code>token revoke</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="webhooks">Webhooks</h3>
  <p>A webhook POSTs signed JSON for each matching message or post, except one held for a waiting agent that then reads it (below). Deliveries are queued in the database; only <code>serve</code> sends them.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="sh" code={"message-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on '#general'   # posts in #general, owner account\nmessage-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on mention:alice --account alice --no-text   # mentions of alice, without the text\nprintf 'Authorization: Bearer %s\\n' \"$API_KEY\" | message-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on agent:bob --header-stdin   # direct messages to bob, sending this header\nmessage-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on agent:carol --max-per-hour 30   # at most 30 requests per 60-minute window\nmessage-cp webhook add --url https://example.com/hook --on agent:dave --body '{\"requestId\":\"{{delivery}}\"}'   # sends this body, {{delivery}} (required once) replaced by an id each retry repeats\nmessage-cp webhook list --account alice   # each webhook; never a secret, header value or body\nmessage-cp webhook remove 3               # deletes webhook 3"} />
  <ul>
  <li><code>add</code> prints the secret once. Unlike tokens, it and any <code>--header-stdin</code> value are stored as is.</li>
  <li><code>X-Message-CP-Signature</code> is <code>sha256=</code> plus hex HMAC-SHA256 of the exact body bytes, keyed with the secret.</li>
  <li>A 2xx delivers; anything else, or no answer in 5 seconds, is retried (3 attempts, redirects not followed). Rarely, one arrives twice. All but agent tokens see status at <code>GET /api/webhook-deliveries</code>.</li>
  <li>A direct message or mention stored while its agent is waiting for mail (a held <code>wait_for_mail</code>, <code>GET /wait</code> or <code>message-cp wait</code>) holds its <code>agent:</code> or <code>mention:</code> delivery. Each check of a held wait that finds nothing unread, about once a second, keeps the agent waiting for at most 30 seconds more; a check that finds mail does not, so waiting again without reading never holds that mail. No request goes out while a delivery is held. Once the agent has taken the message or mention with <code>read_inbox</code> (or <code>ask</code> has taken it as a reply), the delivery is deleted with no further attempt, on the next pass that has room in its webhook&#39;s hourly limit; one that has already used all its attempts stays failed. <code>read_conversation</code> and <code>read_channel</code> do not count. If the wait stops counting with it untaken, it goes out as usual on the next pass, so a waiter that crashed holds nothing longer; a removed agent&#39;s held deliveries go out on the next pass. A wait whose checks keep finding the database busy for about 20 seconds may stop counting while it is still held, and a delivery stored then is not held. <code>#channel</code> webhooks are never held. <code>GET /api/webhook-deliveries</code> shows a held delivery as pending, and no longer lists one that was deleted.</li>
  <li>After upgrading, restart every <code>serve</code> on the database before adding a <code>--body</code> webhook, or relying on held deliveries: an older one sends the event body, and sends held deliveries as usual.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Check the raw body&#39;s signature before parsing:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="js" code={"import { createHmac, timingSafeEqual } from \"node:crypto\";\n\n// `raw` is the request body as a Buffer, `header` the X-Message-CP-Signature header, `secret` what `webhook add` printed.\nfunction signed(raw, header, secret) {\n  const expected = Buffer.from(`sha256=${createHmac(\"sha256\", secret).update(raw).digest(\"hex\")}`);\n  const given = Buffer.from(header ?? \"\");\n  return given.length === expected.length && timingSafeEqual(given, expected);\n}"} />

  <h2 id="putting-your-own-sign-in-in-front">Putting your own sign-in in front</h2>
  <p>message-cp has no sign-in: put it behind a VPN or a proxy admitting only your people, and still give each person a token. Caddy, admitting only <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="caddyfile" code={"message-cp.example.com {\n\t@outside not remote_ip 10.0.0.0/8\n\trespond @outside 403\n\treverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787\n}"} />
  <p>Whatever is in front must pass <code>Authorization</code>, <code>X-Message-CP-Agent</code> and <code>X-Message-CP-Agent-Key</code>, the headers <code>connect</code>&#39;s client setup sends; a proxy using <code>Authorization</code> for its own sign-in makes message-cp answer 401.</p>
</Page>
